<h2>Where homeowner claims usually come up short</h2>
<p>Idaho’s housing stock is unusually varied. A single week of claims can include a 1910s brick bungalow in Boise’s North End, a 2019 two-story in Kuna with a 6/12 roof, a log cabin above McCall and a manufactured home on acreage near Jerome. Estimating software handles all of them with the same templates, and that is where gaps appear.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Partial-slope roof repairs</strong> when the shingle line is discontinued and a patch can’t be matched.</li>
<li><strong>Interior finishes priced as builder-grade</strong> in homes that were upgraded.</li>
<li><strong>Code items left off</strong>, such as ice-and-water shield at the eaves or updated venting required when a roof is replaced.</li>
<li><strong>Contractor overhead and profit omitted</strong> on jobs that clearly need a general contractor to coordinate several trades.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation applied too aggressively</strong> to items with plenty of life left.</li>
</ul>
<h2>How we approach a homeowner claim appraisal</h2>
<p>We start with your declarations page, the carrier’s full estimate and your photos. If the gap looks meaningful, we inspect in person and build our own scope, room by room and roof plane by roof plane. You receive a plain-English comparison showing every line where our valuation differs from the carrier’s and why.</p>
<p>Many claims settle at that point through a documented supplement. When they don’t, and the policy has an appraisal clause, we can serve as your named appraiser and take the file to a panel.</p>
<h2>Condos, rentals and second homes</h2>
<p>Condo owners in Boise and Coeur d’Alene often deal with two policies: the association’s master policy and their own HO-6. Landlords in Rexburg and Moscow carry dwelling-fire forms with different settlement terms. Second-home owners in Blaine and Valley counties may face vacancy conditions after a frozen-pipe loss. We read the specific form before we give an opinion, because the answer changes with the policy.</p>

<h2>Questions about homeowner claim appraisal</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Do I need a contractor’s estimate before calling?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>It helps, but it isn’t required. We can start with the carrier’s estimate and your photos and tell you whether an independent scope is worth the effort.</p></div></details><details><summary>My claim was paid months ago. Is it too late?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Not necessarily. Policies set time limits for disputes and lawsuits, and those vary. Check the ‘Suit Against Us’ and appraisal conditions in your policy, and call us or an Idaho attorney promptly.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will appraisal raise my premium?<svg class="chev" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false"><path d="M6 9l6 6 6-6"/></svg></summary><div class="faq-a"><p>Appraisal is a policy condition, not a separate claim. Premiums depend on the carrier’s underwriting, and we can’t predict those decisions.</p></div></details></div>
